<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Tercia pars regni</h1><p class="st-meta">1048 CE – 1107 CE (approx.) · capital Nyitra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ducatus, a third of the Hungarian kingdom ruled by princes of the Árpád dynasty, which fuelled civil wars until abolished by Coloman.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1048 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-i-of-hungary">Andrew I</a> granted a third of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1000-1301) to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-i-of-hungary">Béla</a>, as a ducatus, or duchy. It covered two blocks: one around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nyitra</a> (Nitra) in the north-west and one around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in the east.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duke held royal revenues, counties and castles in his third, struck his own coins and kept his own retinue. The arrangement made the duke heir presumptive, but it repeatedly led to war: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-i-of-hungary">Béla</a> took the throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-i-of-hungary">Andrew</a> in 1060, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-i-of-hungary">Géza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-i-of-hungary">Ladislaus</a> fought King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> in the 1070s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>King Coloman ended the arrangement, taking the duchy from his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia">Álmos</a> in 1107. The territory returned to the crown, and Álmos was later blinded with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-i-of-hungary">Béla</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1048 CE – 1107 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.56%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1048 CE – 1107 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nyitra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Béla I, Géza I, Álmos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus (Hungary), Hungarian ducatus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=tercia-pars-regni" aria-label="Tercia pars regni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5127 483 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tercia pars regni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5447 739h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5447 739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Slovakia. <a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=tercia-pars-regni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

